<figure class=3D"alignwide"><table style=3D"background-color: #f5fcf5; bord=
er-left:5px solid #baeaba; font-size:small;"><tbody>
<tr><td style=3D"padding: 5px;">Post is a part of a larger series (Creases)=
:<br>
<ul>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/hinge-crease/">Hinge crease</a></=
li>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/ridge-crease/">Ridge crease</a></=
li>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/axial-creases/">Axial creases</a>=
</li>
</ul>
</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p>Axial crease is one of the three basic origami creases. By definition <s=
trong>Axial creases are creases that run in the direction that is perpendic=
ular to the polygon=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/hinge-c=
rease/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer noopener">hinge creases</a></st=
rong>.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Simplest form of a axial creases</h2>



<p>In most basic form, meaning in the models that do not use more advanced =
origami techniques such as level shifters or Pythagorean stretch, axial cre=
ases are always horizontal or vertical, fully coinciding with lines that ma=
ke up the grid.</p>



<p>Let me show how this looks in a simple example. In figure 2, we have one=
 such example.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S2.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern example" class=3D"=
wp-image-521"><figcaption>Figure 1</figcaption></figure>



<p>As you can see, all <a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/ridge-crease/"=
 target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer noopener">ridge creases</a> are red wh=
ile all hinge creases (boundaries of a polygon) are orange.<br>Now, let=E2=
=80=99s analyse, for example, polygon A. If we want to add axial creases to=
 this polygon, we must draw lines that are perpendicular to the hinge creas=
es. At the same time, these axial creases must coincide with lines that mak=
e up the grid.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S3.png" alt=3D"Axial creases are always perpendi=
cular to the ridge crease." class=3D"wp-image-522"><figcaption>Figure 2</fi=
gcaption></figure>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Interaction with a <a href=3D"https://abrash=
iorigami.com/ridge-crease/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer noopener">r=
idge crease</a></h2>



<p>In figure 2, you can see something interesting. All vertical and horizon=
tal axial creases will collide at the polygon=E2=80=99s ridge crease locati=
on. That brings us to one of the most important rules concerning axial crea=
ses. <strong>Axial crease must change direction whenever it intersects ridg=
e crease</strong>. And it must do that respecting the following rule: a<str=
ong>xial crease must reflect across the ridge crease</strong>. In other wor=
ds, the angle at which the axial crease hits the ridge crease is the same a=
ngle at which it will leave the ridge crease. If you are a bit confused, lo=
ok at figure 4.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"285" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S4.png" alt=3D"Axial crease reflecting over the =
ridge crease." class=3D"wp-image-523"><figcaption>Figure 3</figcaption></fi=
gure>



<p>s you can see an angle a is the same as an angle b. In this particular c=
ase, since this model is a simple one and all ridge creases run at a 45-deg=
ree angle, all axial creases upon hitting a ridge crease will turn at a 90-=
degree angle. But do not be mistaken, this rule is universal, meaning it st=
ands for all angles. Remember that.</p>



<p>An example shown in figure 3 is a classical one, and in most cases, the =
axial crease will reflect across a ridge crease as shown in figure 4. But, =
there are three exceptions. Again, do not be mistaken, the fact that we are=
 talking about special cases does not mean that the above-mentioned rule do=
es not apply. It does, believe me.</p>



<p>First of these special cases is shown in figure 4.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"256" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S5.png" alt=3D"Interaction bet=
ween ridge and axial crease (special case no. 1)" class=3D"wp-image-524"><f=
igcaption>Figure 4</figcaption></figure>



<p>As you can see, the axial crease hits the intersection point between two=
 ridge creases. In this particular case, the axial crease should bounce bac=
k into the direction it came from.</p>



<p>How do we know that? Well, let=E2=80=99s think about it. If we move the =
axial crease a few centimetres to the left it will hit ridge crease B. Then=
 it would reflect over it turning right at a 90-degree angle hitting anothe=
r ridge crease (ridge crease A). Again, the axial crease would reflect over=
 that ridge crease too, turning downward into the direction it came from.</=
p>



<p>Now, if we start to move the axial crease incrementally to the right, it=
 will always bounce back into the direction it came from. Only the distance=
 between the incoming and the returning axial crease is going to be smaller=
 and smaller. In the extreme case, when the axial crease hits the ridge cre=
ases=E2=80=99 intersection point, the distance between incoming and returni=
ng axial crease will disappear. Meaning, the axial crease will bounce back =
as I have told you.</p>



<p>In figure 5, we can see another such case.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1015" height=3D"273" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S6.png" alt=3D"Interaction bet=
ween ridge and axial crease (special case no. 2)" class=3D"wp-image-525"><f=
igcaption>ure 5</figcaption></figure>



<p>As you can see the axial crease again hits the intersection point betwee=
n two ridge creases but, this time it does reflect across both of them effe=
ctively splitting itself into two axial creases. Please, pay attention to t=
he fact that the axial crease hits the ridge crease C at 90-degree angle. T=
he result is an axial crease that keeps going forward as if there is no rid=
ge crease in front of it.</p>



<p>All of this brings us to yet another rule. All axial creases are, in ess=
ence, continuous lines that march across the crease pattern until they run =
out of the paper, come back to their point of origin or end in the intersec=
tion point between two ridge creases (case no. 1).</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Defining all axial creases on a crease patte=
rn</h2>



<p>Now, that we are familiar with the basic rules, let=E2=80=99s try to def=
ine all axial creases in a simple example. For the beginning, let=E2=80=99s=
 start with a crease pattern in figure 2. This one is interesting since it =
does not implement advanced origami technique (there are no Pythagorean str=
etch nor level shifters).</p>



<p>In a simple example, like this one, on one side of a ridge crease, an ax=
ial crease is always horizontal, and on the other side, it is always vertic=
al. There cannot be both types of axial creases on the same side of a ridge=
 crease.<br>A direct consequence of this rule is that on the crease pattern=
 there are distinct sectors of only horizontal or only vertical axial lines=
. The lines that unambiguously mark the transition from one sector to anoth=
er are none but ridge creases.</p>



<p>So let=E2=80=99s divide the sectors bounded by the ridge crease in two g=
roups. To make the whole process easier, we will colour these sectors in tw=
o colours: orange and blue. Bear in mind that two sectors cannot be coloure=
d the same if they are next to each other. That=E2=80=99s why we need to co=
lour our crease pattern so that it resembles a chessboard.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S7.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern =
divided into the=20
sectors bounded by ridge crease." class=3D"wp-image-526"><figcaption>Figure=
 6</figcaption></figure>



<p>Look at our crease pattern in figure 6. There are no sectors of the same=
 colour touch one another. If we somehow found two sectors that do, then th=
ere is something wrong with our design. Most likely with ridge creases. The=
re is definitively a mistake somewhere.</p>



<p>This procedure allows us to almost automatically divide the crease patte=
rn into sectors of horizontal and sectors of vertical axial creases. But ag=
ain, there is one small problem. We still don=E2=80=99t know which colour, =
blue or orange, represents horizontal and which colour represents vertical =
axial creases. Be very careful with that. It is not all the same.</p>



<p>To solve this problem, we only need to define the direction of axial cre=
ases in just one of these sectors. To do so, we have to pick one of these s=
ectors and find a hinge crease in it. Then, all we have to do is to draw al=
l axial creases that are perpendicular to that hinge crease and at the same=
 time that coincide with the grid. And that=E2=80=99s it.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S8.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern.
Axial creases in the first sector." class=3D"wp-image-527"><figcaption>Figu=
re 7</figcaption></figure>



<p>At this moment we know the direction of all axial creases. Blue sectors =
contain horizontal while orange sectors contain vertical axial creases. Now=
, we can define all axial creases, by drawing horizontal lines in the blue =
sectors and vertical lines in orange sectors.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"429" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S9.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern =
and all axial creases." class=3D"wp-image-528"><figcaption>Figure 8</figcap=
tion></figure>



<p>That was easy, would you agree?</p>



<p>But, please be aware that this simplified approach is possible since thi=
s example is simple. It does not contain any of the more advanced elements.=
 Meaning, all hinge creases are either horizontal or vertical.</p>



<p>Before we move on to the more complex cases, I would like to underline y=
et another future of the axial creases. All axial creases are, in essence, =
continuous lines that march across the crease pattern until they run out of=
 the paper, come back to their point of origin or end in the intersection p=
oint between two ridge creases (case no. 1).</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"429" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S10.png" alt=3D"Axial creases =
as a continuous line. " class=3D"wp-image-529"><figcaption>Figure 9</figcap=
tion></figure>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">More complex cases</h2>



<p>There are, of course, crease patterns with more asymmetrical polygons, t=
he polygons that are bounded by hinge creases that run at various angles. B=
ut even in such cases basic rules still apply.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S11.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern=
 example" class=3D"wp-image-530"><figcaption>Crease pattern exampleFigure 1=
0</figcaption></figure>



<p>Look at figure 10. Two polygons (A and B) are highly asymmetrical since =
their edges (or hinge creases) do not necessarily run horizontally or verti=
cally (just for your information, this kind of polygon shape is a result of=
 the implementation of a technique called Pythagorean stretch).</p>



<p>Even in such cases, it is possible to divide the whole crease pattern in=
to the distinct sectors bounded by ridge creases.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S12.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern=
 divided into the=20
sectors bounded by ridge crease." class=3D"wp-image-531"><figcaption>Figure=
 11</figcaption></figure>



<p>gain, as the basic rule implies, each of these sectors should hold only =
a set of axial creases of the unique direction.<br>So, let=E2=80=99s start.=
<br>It would be wise to start with sectors that include the hinge crease th=
at coincides with the grid. So, let us start with the sector (1). In that c=
ase, everything is the same as in our previous example. We just have to dra=
w all lines (or axial creases) that are perpendicular to the hinge crease (=
orange line) and at the same time coincide with the grid. The same applies =
to sectors (2), (4) and (5). They all have a hinge crease that coincides wi=
th the grid.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S13.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern=
 with sector 3 market in green." class=3D"wp-image-532"><figcaption>Figure =
12</figcaption></figure>



<p>With axial creases in the sector (3) (one in the middle), you should be =
careful. You see these axial creases should be perpendicular to the hinge c=
rease H. That is obvious. But, since these axial creases do not coincide wi=
th the grid, we have to be careful while positioning them. The difficulty i=
s that they should be in line with the axial creases of adjacent sectors. T=
hey should be positioned as if they are just extensions of axial creases in=
 adjacent sectors. They should look as if they are the same axial creases.<=
/p>



<p>If it is so, then the solution is quite simple. We have to extend the ax=
ial crease from adjacent sectors into the sector C, having in mind that axi=
al crease upon hitting ridge crease should reflect across that ridge crease=
 effectively changing direction. Their new direction must be if we did ever=
ything right, perpendicular to the hinge crease H.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large is-style-default"><img loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1021" height=3D"428" src=3D"https://a=
brashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/S14.png" alt=3D"Crease pattern=
 and all axial creases." class=3D"wp-image-533"></figure>



<p>That would be all for this blog post. I hope the explanations were clear=
 and understandable.</p>
